/*!
    \page qtros2-teleoperation.html
    \title Teleoperation and Remote Control
    \ingroup qtros2-guides

    \keyword joystick
    \keyword gamepad
    \keyword remote control
    \keyword teleoperation
    \keyword velocity command
    \keyword cmd_vel
    \keyword twist
    \keyword on-screen joystick
    \keyword virtual joystick
    \keyword differential drive
    \keyword holonomic
    \keyword deadman switch
    \keyword deadman button

    \brief How to read joystick input and publish velocity commands from QML.

    Remote control of a robot from a Qt application involves two message types:

    \list
        \li \l joy (Joy) — raw axis and button state from a physical gamepad or
            joystick, published by the \c joy_node driver.
        \li \l twist (Twist) — a linear and angular velocity command sent to
            the robot, typically on the \c /cmd_vel topic.
    \endlist

    A Qt application can sit anywhere in this chain:
    it can subscribe to \c Joy and re-publish \c Twist after applying your own mapping,
    or it can bypass \c Joy entirely and publish \c Twist directly from an on-screen
    virtual joystick.

    \section1 Velocity Commands: the Twist Message

    \l twist (Twist) carries two three-dimensional vectors:

    \table
    \header \li Field \li Type \li Meaning
    \row \li \c linear.x \li m/s \li Forward (+) / backward (−)
    \row \li \c linear.y \li m/s \li Strafe left (+) / right (−) — holonomic robots only
    \row \li \c linear.z \li m/s \li Up (+) / down (−) — aerial vehicles only
    \row \li \c angular.x \li rad/s \li Roll — aerial vehicles only
    \row \li \c angular.y \li rad/s \li Pitch — aerial vehicles only
    \row \li \c angular.z \li rad/s \li Turn left (+) / right (−)
    \endtable

    For a typical differential-drive robot, only \c linear.x (drive forward/back) and
    \c angular.z (steer) are used.  All other components should be zero.

    \section2 TwistStamped

    Some robot drivers — in particular the ROS 2 Nav2 stack and many newer hardware
    interfaces — require \l twistStamped (TwistStamped) instead of plain
    \c Twist.  The only difference is a \c std_msgs/Header field that carries a timestamp
    and frame ID.  Check your robot's documentation or the value of the
    \c robot_description parameter to see which topic type it expects.

    \section1 Subscribing to a Physical Joystick

    The \c joy package (installed separately from the Qt ROS2 Bridge) provides
    \c joy_node, which reads a Linux input device and publishes on \c /joy:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.SensorMsgs

    JoySubscriber {
        id: joyInput
        topic: "/joy"
        node: myNode
        onMessageReceived: (msg) => handleJoy(msg)
    }
    \endqml

    The \l joy (Joy) message contains:

    \list
        \li \c axes — \c float32[] normalised to −1.0…+1.0.  The mapping from array
            index to physical stick axis is controller-dependent; most gamepads follow the
            convention left-stick-X=0, left-stick-Y=1, triggers=2/5,
            right-stick-X=3, right-stick-Y=4.
        \li \c buttons — \c int32[] with 0 (released) or 1 (pressed).
    \endlist

    \section2 Mapping Joy to Twist

    Translate axis values into a \c Twist and publish on \c /cmd_vel:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs
    import QtRos2.SensorMsgs

    Node { id: myNode; nodeName: "qt_teleop" }

    JoySubscriber {
        topic: "/joy"
        node: myNode
        onMessageReceived: (joy) => {
            // Axis 1: left stick Y — forward/back.  Axis 0: left stick X — turn.
            // Invert Y because stick-up is typically negative on most gamepads.
            const deadman = joy.buttons[4] === 1  // left shoulder button
            const vx = deadman ? -joy.axes[1] * maxLinear  : 0
            const wz = deadman ?  joy.axes[0] * maxAngular : 0
            drivePublisher.publishVelocity(vx, wz)
        }
    }

    TwistPublisher {
        id: drivePublisher
        topic: "/cmd_vel"
        node: myNode

        property real maxLinear:  0.5  // m/s
        property real maxAngular: 1.0  // rad/s

        function publishVelocity(vx, wz) {
            var msg = Qt.createQmlObject("import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs; Twist {}", this)
            msg.linear.x  = vx
            msg.angular.z = wz
            publish(msg)
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 On-Screen Virtual Joystick

    When no physical gamepad is available, a \c MultiPointTouchArea (or \c MouseArea on
    desktop) can act as a virtual joystick.  The principle is the same: map a normalised
    2D offset into \c Twist fields and publish while the user is touching the control.

    \qml
    import QtQuick
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs

    Node { id: myNode; nodeName: "qt_teleop" }

    TwistPublisher {
        id: cmdVel
        topic: "/cmd_vel"
        node: myNode
    }

    // Joystick pad: a fixed circle with a movable thumb indicator
    Rectangle {
        id: pad
        width: 160; height: 160
        radius: 80
        color: "#44ffffff"

        property real thumbX: 0  // −1.0 … +1.0
        property real thumbY: 0  // −1.0 … +1.0

        // Thumb indicator
        Rectangle {
            width: 40; height: 40; radius: 20
            color: "white"
            x: (pad.width  - width)  / 2 + pad.thumbX * (pad.width  / 2 - 20)
            y: (pad.height - height) / 2 - pad.thumbY * (pad.height / 2 - 20)
        }

        MultiPointTouchArea {
            anchors.fill: parent
            maximumTouchPoints: 1

            touchPoints: [ TouchPoint { id: tp } ]

            onUpdated: {
                const cx = pad.width  / 2
                const cy = pad.height / 2
                const dx = (tp.x - cx) / cx
                const dy = (cy - tp.y) / cy  // invert: up is positive
                // Clamp to unit circle
                const len = Math.min(1.0, Math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy))
                const angle = Math.atan2(dy, dx)
                pad.thumbX = len * Math.cos(angle)
                pad.thumbY = len * Math.sin(angle)
                publishFromThumb()
            }

            onReleased: {
                pad.thumbX = 0
                pad.thumbY = 0
                publishFromThumb()  // send zero — see Safety note below
            }
        }

        function publishFromThumb() {
            var msg = Qt.createQmlObject("import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs; Twist {}", pad)
            msg.linear.x  = thumbY * 0.5   // forward/back, max 0.5 m/s
            msg.angular.z = -thumbX * 1.0  // turn, max 1.0 rad/s
            cmdVel.publish(msg)
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \section2 Split-axis Layout

    Many teleoperation UIs place two separate one-axis sliders: one for throttle
    (forward/back, \c linear.x) and one for steering (turn, \c angular.z), matching the
    feel of a radio-control car transmitter.  The same \c TwistPublisher approach applies;
    only the axis mapping changes.

    \section1 Safety: the Deadman Switch

    A robot receiving a sustained non-zero \c Twist will keep moving if the
    controlling application crashes, loses network connectivity, or its window loses focus.
    Always implement a deadman mechanism:

    \list
        \li \b{Physical gamepad}: only publish non-zero velocity while a designated button
            (the "deadman button") is held.  On button release, publish one explicit zero
            Twist before stopping publication.
        \li \b{On-screen joystick}: publish a zero Twist in the \c onReleased handler, as
            shown above.  Also publish zero in \c Component.onDestruction and whenever the
            enclosing \c Window loses \c activeFocus.
        \li \b{Velocity limits}: clamp \c Twist values to safe maximums before publishing.
            Never pass raw axis values multiplied by an arbitrarily large scale factor.
        \li \b{Watchdog on the robot}: ideally the robot firmware or base driver should
            apply its own timeout — stopping automatically if no \c Twist arrives within
            a configured interval (e.g. 500 ms).  This guards against application crashes
            and network outages regardless of what the UI does.
    \endlist

    \section1 Navigation Goals (Click-to-Navigate)

    Sending a single goal pose to an autonomous navigation stack — rather than continuously
    streaming velocity — uses a different message type:

    \list
        \li Publish a \l poseStamped (PoseStamped) on \c /goal_pose.
            Nav2 picks this up, plans a path, and drives the robot autonomously.
        \li The pose's \c header.frame_id must match the map frame (typically \c "map").
        \li The orientation quaternion encodes the desired heading at the goal; leave it as
            the identity quaternion if the final heading does not matter.
    \endlist

    This approach is complementary to direct velocity teleoperation: a Qt UI can let the
    user switch between "manual drive" mode (Twist) and "click destination on the map"
    mode (PoseStamped).

    \section1 Haptic Feedback

    Physical gamepads that support rumble or LED colour can receive feedback via
    \l joyFeedbackArray (JoyFeedbackArray).  Publish an array of
    \l joyFeedback (JoyFeedback) entries on \c /joy/set_feedback:

    \list
        \li \c type: 0 = LED, 1 = rumble, 2 = buzzer
        \li \c id: selects which LED or motor when the device has several
        \li \c intensity: 0.0 (off) … 1.0 (full)
    \endlist

    This is useful for alerting the operator when the robot reaches a goal, encounters
    an obstacle, or the battery is low.

    \sa {Choosing Sensor Message Types}
*/
